 ### Indirect Submission
-Indirect submission is a model where the CPU emits all GPU commands, but the parameters of said commands are filled by the GPU. The command contains an address in GPU memory, which the GPU reads before execution to determine parameters [S1, unofficial]. A first pass writes to the pointer in GPU memory to decide the command's parameters. A barrier must be placed between the two passes to ensure correct execution [S1, unofficial]. Task size can be set to zero to effectively cancel a submission, but the submission overhead is still incurred [S1, unofficial]. It is not possible to dynamically create new submissions from the GPU. If the number of required passes is not known in advance, one must issue indirect submissions for a maximum number of passes and dynamically cancel unnecessary ones, paying overhead many times [S1, unofficial]. It is an awkward hybrid model where the GPU controls most aspects but cannot initiate submissions, making it unintuitive and error-prone [S1, unofficial]. Widely available and in use today for indirect control of task size (number of workgroups, vertices, rays). Advanced iterations include DirectX ExecuteIndirect and Vulkan VK_EXT_device_generated_commands [S1, unofficial].
